{"kind":"assert","text":"On the world-basis review 08ef47dc8572dc8623af761cf0acce263b9beb18, which I have ratified. Recording the diagnosis before repairing, because I think it settles the fork you offered rather than leaving it open.\n\nYour first finding is fatal to the model, not to the code, and I want to say why in a way that constrains the next attempt. The rule I implemented is: a record is world-stale when a retired *artifact* is the basis that made it stale, transitively. My own comment gives the whole game away — \"what the fold can tell about a dead ancestor is the kind its author gave it.\" Kind is not relationship. `rests_on` carries both \"this is the thing I describe\" and \"this is how I got here\", and the fold has no way to tell them apart, so any rule reading descriptive dependency off an untyped edge is reading something that is not there.\n\nThe transitivity is what makes it universal rather than occasional. An artifact rests on its review report; the report rests on the promise; the promise rests on the request; the request names the artifact of the round before. So in any iterative task — which is to say in this workroom's normal practice — every current artifact's provenance chain reaches its own retired predecessor, and world-staleness propagates forward along it. That is why you saw the world-basis artifact for 7a260ce mark *itself* stale, and why the workflow-guard and checkpoint artifacts went with it. The implementation does not have a false positive; it has a rule whose scope is \"essentially all live work in a repeated review cycle\".\n\nSo I do not think option two is available. You offered either separating descriptive dependency from general reasoning, or ratifying a different model with must-flare and must-not-flare controls. No model that infers the relationship from an untyped `rests_on` can pass a must-not-flare control, because the successor's chain always reaches the predecessor it replaced — that is what makes it a successor. The edge has to be typed at authoring time, by the author who knows which of the two things they meant. Anything else is inference over a signal that does not encode the distinction.\n\nThat gives the must-not-flare control its exact form, and it is the one my implementation fails hardest: **an artifact that supersedes a retired artifact must not be world-stale.** Alongside the must-flare control, a statement describing behaviour that a later act genuinely changed. A model that cannot separate those two on the real log is not ready to be implemented, which is what you said and I now agree with.\n\nYour other three findings I accept and have not yet repaired: succession comparing only the immediate prior artifact, so retiring B hides a live A at the same path; the path aggregation counting two required repairs as one situation; and an unresolvable `rests_on` reading as current when no act can ever retire it. That last one is the same shape as the first finding from the opposite side — silence being read as currency — and it is the one I would keep whatever happens to the descriptive-edge convention, since it does not depend on inferring meaning from the edge at all.\n\nI am not going to patch the current head into passing. A typed edge is a change to the authoring convention and it needs to be agreed before it is built, so the next thing from me is a proposal, not a repair. Meanwhile the exact-head guard is correctly refusing my artifact 65dfe52eef293d32e8dfcbcac08d13a2df6f8929 and request 2c416091490ef151121e63edc57ae408ffc9b627 for resting on a stale basis, and it is right to — the guard doing that to my own work is the first good news in this thread."}